<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap-text wrap-text--intro center">
    <p class="eyebrow">For everyone</p>
    <h1>The lizards of Miami</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Miami has, entirely by accident, become one of the best places in the world
      to watch ecology and evolution happen.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section--tight">
  <div class="wrap-text">
    <p>
      Over the past century, tropical lizards have arrived in south Florida from
      Cuba, Hispaniola, Puerto Rico, Jamaica, and Central America, carried in
      plant shipments and cargo. The result is a community of species that never
      encountered one another in their evolutionary histories, now living
      together in the same parks, gardens, and back yards, and sorting out their
      relationships in real time.
    </p>
    <p>
      To an evolutionary biologist this is an experiment nobody could have been
      funded to run. We know roughly when each species arrived. We can watch what
      happens next. When two species meet for the first time, do they diverge? Do
      they change how they use the habitat, or what they look like? How quickly?
      Those are the questions the lab exists to answer, and Miami is where the
      answers are.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--band">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="split">
      <figure>
        <img src="/assets/img/photos/carolinensis-grass.jpg" alt="A green anole on a blade of grass">
        <figcaption><em>Anolis carolinensis</em>, the green anole.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div>
        <h2>The one that was already here</h2>
        <p>
          The green anole, <em>Anolis carolinensis</em>, is the only anole native
          to the southeastern United States. Its ancestors reached Florida from
          Cuba roughly 5 to 7 million years ago, and it now ranges as far north as
          Tennessee and North Carolina.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you grew up in the southeast and remember green lizards on the porch
          that seem scarcer now, you are not imagining it, though the story is more
          interesting than simple displacement. Where brown anoles arrive, green
          anoles tend to move upward into the canopy rather than disappear.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="center">Some species you are likely to meet</h2>
    <div class="cards" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <div class="card">
        <p class="card__kicker">Native</p>
        <h3>Green anole</h3>
        <p><em>Anolis carolinensis</em>. Green or brown depending on mood and
        temperature, with a pink dewlap. Usually higher up than you are looking.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="card__kicker">From Cuba</p>
        <h3>Brown anole</h3>
        <p><em>Anolis sagrei</em>. The most abundant lizard in Miami by a wide
        margin. Brown, patterned, low on trunks and on the ground, with an orange
        and yellow dewlap.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="card__kicker">From Puerto Rico</p>
        <h3>Crested anole</h3>
        <p><em>Anolis cristatellus</em>. Slightly larger than a brown anole, with
        a raised tail crest in males. Where it arrives in Miami, it generally dominates
        brown anoles and causes them to perch lower to the ground and in hotter locations.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="card__kicker">From Hispaniola</p>
        <h3>Bark anole</h3>
        <p><em>Anolis distichus</em>. A small anole, these lizards are lightning quick
        and specialize living on broad tree trunks. They predominantly eat only ants,
        and will 'squirrel' to the other side of a tree as soon as you see them!</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="card__kicker">From Cuba</p>
        <h3>Knight anole</h3>
        <p><em>Anolis equestris</em>. The giant of the group, bright green and up
        to a foot and a half long. It generally lives high in tree canopus, eats a wide 
        range of food items--including both fruit and other lizards, including smaller anoles!</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--band">
  <div class="wrap-text center">
    <h2>Take part</h2>
    <p>
      Every anole you photograph and upload is a data point. More than 10,000
      verified records have already come from middle school students in the
      Lizards on the Loose programme, and the community can always use more.
    </p>
    <p>
      Photograph the lizard, upload it to <a href="https://www.inaturalist.org">iNaturalist</a>,
      and let the community confirm the identification. Several of these species
      look near-identical to a beginner, which is exactly why we built
      <a href="/software/">LizardLens</a> to help.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-top:2rem">
      <a class="btn btn--accent" href="/outreach/">About Lizards on the Loose</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

